# 1 The Minor Miss: The OnInit Clamp
You updated the button cycle to max out at 40, but the hardcoded clamp in OnInit was missed.


The Issue: Currently, OnInit checks if(mul > 100) mul = 100;. If a user types 80 into the InpTickMultiplier input parameter, the indicator will accept it, which breaks the synchronization with your UI button that expects a max of 40.

The Fix: Simply change line 422 to if(mul > 40) mul = 40; to keep the input settings mathematically aligned with your UI.


# 2 Advanced Edge-Case (The "Static" Trap)
There is one hidden procedural danger introduced by the ProcessTicks optimization that you should be aware of.

Because you declared current_bar_time, current_sh, and next_bar_time as static inside ProcessTicks, they remember their values across entirely different calls.

The Risk: If you change the chart timeframe from M1 to H1, ProcessTicks is called to rebuild the history. The very first tick it processes might mathematically fall inside the old M1 static boundaries remaining in memory. If it does, ProcessTicks skips iBarShift and assigns the tick to the wrong timeframe's bar index.

The Fix: You need a way to reset these static variables when ReloadHistory is triggered. Since we are avoiding OOP, the cleanest procedural way is to add a bool reset_cache = false parameter to ProcessTicks.